[1B[1mFour grains, four names.[28G[22mPlexus[35Gnames[41Gthe[45Gepisode,[54Gheart[60Gsplits[67Git[70Ginto[75Gsubtasks[84Gand[88Gassigns[96Ga[98Grun[102Gid[105Gper[109Gsubtask,[118Gdocker-sbx[129Gcontains[138Gthe[142Gepisode,[151Garteries[160Gremembers[170Geach[175Gsubtask[183Gseparately.[195GThe[199Gcontainer[209Gis[212Gthe[216Gonly[221Gone[225Gof[228Gthe[232Gfour[237Gwith[242Gno[245Gidentity[2C[1Bof its own — it's a runtime, not a scope. That's Miss[57Gon Control's harde[77G-won lesson: [91Ghey modeled[103Gsandboxes a[116Gproject scopes, a[134Gd[136Gh[138Gd to undo it.[K[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mThe container wraps the episode, not the subtask.[53G[22mSubtasks[62Galready[70Gshare[76Ga[78Gfilesystem[89Gby[92Gdesign[99G—[101Gthe[105Gfix[109Gagent[115Gneeds[121Gwhat[126Gthe[130Gcoder[136Gwrote.[143GAnd[147Gper-subtask[159Gcontainers[170Gbuy[174Gno[177Gsecurity,[187Gbecause[195Gevery[201Gsubtask[209Ghas[213Gidentical[223Gpermissions[235Gagainst[243Gthe[247Gsame[2C[1Bcode. The surface that matters is[37Gcontainer-to-host, and that's the sa[74Ge eith[82G way. Warren reached the same plac[118Gindepe[125Gdently: [3m"isolation at the run granularity, not per-task or per-session."[23m[K[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mContext is pushed in, never pulled out.[43G[22mThis[48Gis[51Gthe[55Gpiece[61Gthat[66Gmakes[72Gleast[78Gprivilege[88Gactually[97Gwork.[103GHeart[109Gbuilds[116Gthe[120Gpacket[127Gand[131Gruns[136Gcapillaries'[149Gretrieval[159Gon[162Gthe[166Ghost,[172Gthen[177Gmounts[184Gthe[188Gresult[195Gread-only.[206GThe[210Gsubtask[218Greads[224Ga[226Gfile[231Gand[235Ghas[239Gno[242Gidea[247Ga[2C[1Bdatabase exists. Writes go out through the spool as appended NDJSON — the[77Gchannel alre[90Gdy exists, it just is[112G't lo[118Gd-bearing yet.[K[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mThe sharpest hazard in your setup[37G[22mis[40G[38;2;177;185;249m/var/run/postgresql[39m.[61GPeer[66Gauth,[72Gempty[78Guser.[84GMount[90Git[93Gso[96Garteries[105Gworks[111Ginside[118Gthe[122Gcontainer[132Gand[136Gyou've[143Ghanded[150Gthe[154Gagent[160Gyour[165Gentire[172Gmemory[179Gstore.[186GThat[191Gmount[197Gis[200Gthe[204Gsingle[211Gmost[216Gtempting[225Gshortcut[234Gand[238Gthe[242Gone[246Gthat[1B [3Gvoids everything else.[K[2B  [1mOne real consequence for code we already changed.[22m [38;2;177;185;249mlog_event[39m falls back to JSONL and swallows the failure — inside a locked-down container that writes into a filesystem that dies with it. Silent total loss of every subtask's memory. The spool has to[K[1B  become the primary write path for sandboxed runs before [38;2;177;185;249mdocker-sbx[39m is worth having. That's item 3 in the build order, and it's the one I'd start on.[K[1B[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m✻[3GCrunched for 3m 50s · 2 shells still running[1B[39m[K[1B[48;2;55;55;55m[38;2;80;80;80m❯ [38;2;255;255;255mOne episode can take longer so cap it at an hour instead, change workspace diffs to actual commits in heart. If there's a sandbox per episode, can permissions be changed for each spawned sandbox to apply least privilege access? What else needs to [39m     [1B  [38;2;255;255;255mchange in across my stack to ensure that sandboxes don't conflict? Change spool to another name that's descriptive and intuitive for its function. Should worktrees be spawned throughout each episode/subtask since I'm trying to avoid being messy with [39m  [1B  [38;2;255;255;255mbranches while having enough isolation at the same time[39m                                                                                                                                                                                                     [1B[49m[K[1B[38;2;255;255;255m●[3G[39mSeveral[11Gthreads[19Ghere.[25GInvestigating[39Gthe[43Gtwo[47Gcode[52Gchanges[60Gfirst.[1B[K[1B  