ches:[31G2[38G←[40Gthe[44Greal[49Gtarget,[57Gplus[62Gthe[66Gsearching[76Gprocess[2C[1Bbracketed p[15Gt[17Gern matche[28G:  1[33G     ←[40Gonly[45Gthe[49Greal[54Gtarget[2C[2BWorse, they compounded.[27GEach new[36Gl[38Gop m[43Gt[45Ghed not only itself but every e[77Grli[81Gr[83Gloop, so once the first one was[115Gstuck, ever[127G subsequent one[143Gwas guaranteed to stick too.[172GThe[176Goldest[183Ghad[187Gbeen[192Gspinning[201Gfor[205Gabout[211G24[214Ghours.[2C[2B[1mNothing was lost.[22m The pyt[29Gst runs themselves completed norm[63Glly and I[73Gread ever[83G result — those loops were[110Go[112Gly waiting [124Go [3mreport[23m, and by the time e[152Gch one hung I'd already fetched its output [196Gnother way.[K[2C[2B[1mTwo fixes[22m[K[2C[2BThe classic trick is to break[33Gthe[37Gself-match[48Gwith[53Ga[55Gcharacter[65Gclass,[72Gsince[78G[38;2;177;185;249m[p]ytest[87G[39mmatches[95Gthe[99Gstring[106G[38;2;177;185;249mpytest[113G[39mbut[117Gthe[121Gpattern[129Gtext[134Gitself[141Gcontains[150G[38;2;177;185;249m[p]ytest[39m,[160Gwhich[166Gdoesn't:[2C[2B[34muntil[39m ! pgrep -f [31m"[p]ytest tests/"[39m >/dev/null; [34mdo[39m [36msleep[39m 25; [34mdone[39m[K[2C[2BBut the better answer is not to poll at all. Your har[57Gess re-invokes me when [81G background task fin[102Gshe[106G, and I[114Galready had task IDs for every one of those runs. Waiting on a notification costs nothing and can't hang; polling was me duplicating a[K[2C[1Bmechanism[13Gthat[18Galready[26Gexisted[34G—[36Gwhich[42Gis[45Gexactly[53Gthe[57Gkind[62Gof[65Gthing[71GI'd[75Gflag[80Gin[83Ga[85Gcode[90Greview.[2C[2BI'll use the notific[24Gtions from here.[K[2B[38;2;153;153;153m✻[3GSautéed for 1m 6s[39m[K[2B[48;2;55;55;55m[38;2;80;80;80m❯ [38;2;255;255;255mGo back to sandbox gaps, what can be done to ensure each part still works well with the security layer